Do not edit text that the input method rewrites unchanged.

Gboard 12.4 on WebView 134 opens a composition over an existing word by
sending that word as a textupdate for the range it already occupies. This
happens on opening an editor, restoring its input connection, and after a
paste or a suggestion accepted with Tab. The replacement went through the
normal composition edit path even when its text and selection were identical.
It advanced the model version, emitted a content change and could dirty an
otherwise unchanged file and schedule autosave. It could also add an undo
entry with no visible effect: one Undo after pasting left the pasted text
in place. An open undo entry can absorb later replacements, so this is not
one separate undo step per update.

On Android, handle a replacement whose text equals the cached text in its
range through the existing selection-only path, provided the cached selection
is collapsed. A commit over a selected range is still typing, including at
secondary cursors whose selected text differs from the primary selection.
With several cursors, take the new return only if the requested selection
matches the cached selection: it writes no text and touches no cursor state.
If the input method requests a different selection with several cursors,
keep the old composition operation and its cursor fanout. With one cursor,
send the requested move as an explicit keyboard selection while keeping
the input method's buffer window. Keep the old empty-update alternative,
including its existing selection behavior.

Check the range length and bounds, and compare against the cache from before
the event, not the native buffer: the browser has already changed that buffer
when the event arrives. A real same-length replacement must still edit.
The existing update method synchronizes the selection cache. Composition
start, end and the cursor's original composition snapshot are unchanged.
An unchanged caret emits no selection event, so the existing test for typing
during composition is not armed. Genuine input earlier in that composition
remains typing. The single-cursor explicit selection move clears that
indication through its existing event. Actual insertions, deletions and
replacements use the old path, as does a stale-range update whose text is
longer than its range. Nothing here repairs such a range or restarts input.
Other platforms keep their existing behavior.

The added work is a string comparison bounded by the cached input window,
which can span several lines, and a cursor-list lookup when the requested
selection differs. An unchanged word creates no edit or undo data on the new
return. An equal commit over selected text, and a nonempty update that moves
the selection with several cursors, can still edit unchanged text through
the old path. Source checks cover the captured word ranges, a caret inside a
range, the next character, a same-length replacement, a moved caret, undo
after a paste, selected commits and cursor preservation; the existing restart
and initialization cases still pass. Server typechecking and minification
pass.

With this change, 0046 and 0047 built, a saved file that was opened or
tapped into, with nothing typed, kept its modification time and its contents
while the keyboard underlined the word. On an API 29 emulator with Gboard
8.0.4 that held after a first open and three taps in the app's storage and
after an open and a tap in a device folder; on an API 33 emulator with
Gboard 12.4, after a first open and three taps on WebView 149 and after an
open and a tap on WebView 134, the keyboard still sending the equal update
each time. A typed letter moved the time and changed the contents in each of
the four places. One Undo after a paste over underlined helloab restored
helloab in three runs on Gboard 8.0.4, in three on Gboard 12.4 with WebView
149 and in one with WebView 134. With two carets after alpha and bravo and
nothing typed for ten seconds, both lines, both carets and the file stayed
as they were in three runs on Gboard 12.4 with WebView 149, where an editor
built without 0046 to 0048 copied alpha over bravo and saved it in three
runs of three. On an API 34 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 157, a tap
into a word left the file unwritten and one Undo removed a paste, three runs
each.

